<Post title="The interval war" part={5} date="2026-09-17" series="rshunterbtt">
	<p class="tagline">
		The rewrite gave the bridge a persistent connection and a configurable connection
		interval, and the interval is what buys battery life. Then the firmware started
		quietly changing it back — and proving that led through two misread kernel constants,
		a probe of the controller's own event stream, and a very long argument with a device
		that simply wants to talk fast.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the parameter that buys battery</h2>
	<p>
		A BLE connection is not a stream; it is a series of short radio rendezvous, and the gap
		between them is the <strong>connection interval</strong>. Every event costs radio time,
		so a longer interval means less power — at the cost of latency, because a round-trip
		has to wait for the next event. The legal range runs from 7.5 ms to 4 s, and the probing
		in part 2 showed this device happily accepts the 4 s maximum.
	</p>
	<p>
		So the bridge requested the maximum, reasoning that commands are rare and battery is
		scarce. That reasoning is correct, and it produced a bridge that was unusable in exactly
		the way that had started all this: a zone query took long enough for the voice
		assistant to give up and mark the device offline, and the controls were no faster. The
		first task was to measure the actual relationship instead of guessing at it.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>measuring it properly</h2>
	<p>
		The bridge logs a command's receipt and its acknowledgement, so the round-trip is
		directly measurable. To vary the interval on a live link I used the controller's
		own connection-update tool, with a capture running to confirm what was actually
		applied. Same command, same host, one variable:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>connection interval</th><th>command → ack</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>60 ms</td><td>0.50 s</td></tr>
			<tr><td>1000 ms</td><td>~5.2 s</td></tr>
			<tr><td>2000 ms</td><td>~12 s</td></tr>
			<tr><td>4000 ms</td><td>~21 s</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		That is a clean, roughly linear relationship — latency scales with the interval, which
		makes physical sense: a command needs a handful of GATT round-trips, and each one waits
		for an event. The 4 s maximum really is a ~21 s command. So the interval is a genuine
		dial between battery and responsiveness, and the interesting question becomes what
		value to choose — not whether the dial works.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>then the numbers stopped making sense</h2>
	<p>
		A later session produced the opposite result: with the bridge configured for the 4 s
		maximum, commands came back in <strong>0.28 seconds</strong>. Both measurements were
		real. One of them was lying about the connection.
	</p>
	<p>
		I ran a check designed to catch exactly this. If the link is genuinely at 4 s, a command
		sent at a random moment has to wait for the next event — so latencies across many
		commands should be <em>spread</em> from near-zero up to four seconds. If instead every
		command, at every phase, comes back in ~0.3 s, the link cannot possibly be at 4 s. Ten
		commands at deliberately uneven spacing came back flat: 0.26–0.31 s, every time. The
		bridge <em>said</em> "negotiated 4000 ms". The link was running at roughly 50 ms.
	</p>
	<p>
		The lesson landed hard and generalised to everything after it: <strong>the log line that
		says the interval was negotiated is not evidence that the interval is in effect.</strong> The only ground truth is the controller's event stream, cross-checked against measured
		latency.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>who is changing my interval</h2>
	<p>
		A full HCI capture of a fresh connection finally showed the whole argument, in order:
	</p>
	<pre><code>{`LE Connection Complete          ->  48 ms      host brings the link up
LE Connection Update Complete   -> 195 ms      host applies preferred parameters
LE Connection Update Complete   -> 1000 ms     bridge asks for its interval
LE L2CAP: Connection Parameter Update Request  <-- the PERIPHERAL asks for fast
LE Connection Update Complete   ->  48 ms      host honours the peripheral
`}</code></pre>
	<p>
		There it is. The timer itself asks to go back to a fast interval, and BlueZ — correctly,
		per the spec — gives the peripheral what it wants. The vendor firmware wants to be
		responsive, the host politely agrees, and the bridge's carefully-chosen interval is gone
		a second after it was set. The connection was never broken; it was being renegotiated
		out from under the bridge.
	</p>
	<p>
		This also explained months of inconsistency. The peripheral sends that request on its <em>first</em> connection after power-up. Reconnect without rebooting the host, and no
		request arrives — so the bridge's interval sticks, and latency is honestly ~5 s. Reboot
		the host, and the request comes, and the link is fast again. Same settings, opposite
		behaviour, depending on whether the firmware had already made its point.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the first fix: a deliberate reconnect</h2>
	<p>
		If the peripheral only renegotiates on its first connection, the obvious move is to give
		it a first connection to spend, then take the second one for real. Connection setup now
		does a small dance: connect, disconnect, connect, and only then apply the interval. On
		the second connection the firmware has no new objection, and the interval holds.
	</p>
	<p>
		It is a slightly absurd fix — deliberately dropping a connection to make the next one
		stick — and it is not sufficient on its own. The firmware's request is intermittent, not
		deterministic; a reconnect is not guaranteed to be free of it. Which means the bridge
		cannot just set the interval and hope. It has to watch.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>a monitor that had never once worked</h2>
	<p>
		Back in the rewrite (part 3) I had given the bridge an HCI <em>monitor</em>: a raw
		socket onto the controller's event stream, decoding a couple of event types for
		diagnostics. The interval work needed exactly that socket — to see every <code>LE Connection Update Complete</code> and catch the moments the link drifted back
		to fast. So the monitor got a second job: not just logging, but <em>enforcement</em>.
	</p>
	<p>
		There was a small problem. It had never decoded a single event. Two constants were
		wrong, and neither was ever noticed because the module was "only diagnostics":
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>The channel number.</strong> Kernel HCI channel numbering is not stable
			across trees. Mainline uses <code>MONITOR=3</code>; this Raspberry Pi kernel uses <code>MONITOR=2</code> and <code>CONTROL=3</code>. The code was binding channel 1
			(<code>USER</code>), which fails outright — and a naive "fix" to channel 3 binds <em>successfully</em> to the management channel and quietly receives the wrong kind
			of events entirely. Both failure modes look like "no data".
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The packet-type opcode.</strong> The monitor's frame types are a separate
			enum again (<code>EVENT=3</code>, not the intuitively "first" value), so even with
			the right channel the decoder was reading the wrong frames.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		I found both by going to the source rather than to memory — pulling the exact kernel
		headers for the running version and reading the enums — and by dumping raw frame bytes
		until the format was unambiguous. Within an hour of fixing two integers, the monitor was
		seeing the event stream it had been silently deaf to since the day it shipped.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>enforcement</h2>
	<p>
		With events flowing, the guard is simple: on every connection-update event, compare the
		reported interval against the target. If it is off — the firmware won a round — re-apply
		the target. Rate-limited so a device that argues every time cannot spin the bridge, and
		run off-thread so a slow re-apply never stalls the monitor and makes it miss the next
		event.
	</p>
	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		EV["LE Connection Update Complete<br/>(every event)"] --> CMP{"interval == target?"}
		CMP -->|yes| IGN["ignore"]
		CMP -->|no| RL{"within rate limit?"}
		RL -->|yes| IGN
		RL -->|no| RE["re-apply target interval<br/>(off-thread HCI update)"]
		RE --> EV`}</Mermaid>
	<p>
		The proof is a fresh boot of each host with the guard running. Every timer did exactly
		what the firmware wanted — reverted to 50 ms, 60 ms, 198 ms, 200 ms — and every revert
		was caught and reversed:
	</p>
	<pre><code>{`interval changed to 60ms (target 1000ms); re-applying
connection interval updated: requested 1000ms, negotiated 1000ms
interval changed to 50ms (target 1000ms); re-applying
connection interval updated: requested 1000ms, negotiated 1000ms`}</code></pre>
	<p>
		After correction, the same stop command measured 5.2 s on the wire — the interval was
		genuinely, verifiably, in effect. And importantly, the corrections <em>settled</em>:
		one or two per connection, then quiet. This is not a device that renegotiates forever;
		it is a device that makes its point, and then behaves once the central has clearly and
		repeatedly insisted.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>so what interval, in the end</h2>
	<p>
		With the link honest, the dial is a real choice. I settled on <strong>1000 ms</strong>:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>~5 s command latency — slow enough to feel deliberate, fast enough not to feel broken.</li>
		<li><strong>4× fewer</strong> connection events than the 4 s maximum.</li>
		<li><strong>16× fewer</strong> than the firmware's preferred ~60 ms.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		That last number is the one that matters, and it is also the one I can't fully defend
		yet. The honest position, carried over from part 2: the firmware wants to talk at 60 ms,
		the ~10-month battery history was measured at roughly that cadence, and the actual
		battery cost of a persistent connection at any interval is <strong>still
		unmeasured</strong>. 1000 ms is a defensible middle — close to the firmware's own
		preference for responsiveness, and a real reduction in radio duty — and it is now under
		observation in production rather than being argued about on paper.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>what this one taught me</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>A confirmation is not a fact.</strong> "Negotiated 1000 ms" was printed every
			time, including times when the link was demonstrably at 50 ms. The system's own
			report about itself was the least reliable evidence available.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Design the experiment to fail.</strong> The phase sweep — send commands at
			random moments; a real 4 s link <em>must</em> show spread — is what settled it. More
			of the same measurement would just have produced more contradictory averages.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Go to the source for encoding.</strong> I "knew" the channel numbers and
			packet types. I was wrong about both, in ways that failed silently. The kernel
			headers were right there.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Diagnostics that never worked are worse than none.</strong> The monitor sat
			in the codebase, unused and failing, for the entire rewrite — and it happened to be
			the exact tool the next problem needed. Unexercised code is not neutral; it is
			unknown.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The bridge holds its interval now, on all three timers, against a peripheral that would
		prefer otherwise. The remaining unknown is the one the whole exercise was for — whether
		any of this buys meaningful battery life. That answer is going to arrive slowly, as a
		line on a graph, over the next few months.
	</p>
</Post>
